ional Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems, pp. 5026–5033. Cited by: cite151†§5.1 .\nL432:   * Todorov (2006) E. Todorov Linearly-solvable markov decision problems. Advances in neural information processing systems 19. Cited by: cite154†§A.2 , cite155†§3.1 .\nL433:   * Vamplew et al. (2011) P. Vamplew, R. Dazeley, A. Berry, R. Issabekov, and E. Dekker Empirical evaluation methods for multiobjective reinforcement learning algorithms. Machine learning 84 (1), pp. 51–80. Cited by: cite165†§5.2 .\nL434:   * Wan et al. (2025) Z. Wan, Y. Ouyang, L. Xie, F. Fang, H. Zha, and G. Cheng Discrete guidance matching: exact guidance for discrete flow matching. arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.21912. Cited by: cite149†§2 .\nL435:   * Wang et al. (2022) Z. Wang, J. J. Hunt, and M. Zhou Diffusion policies as an expressive policy class for offline reinforcement learning. In The Eleventh International Conference on Learning Representations, Cited by: cite146†§1 , cite147†§2 .\nL436:   * Yuan et al. (2025) Y. Yuan, Z. Zheng, Z. Dong, and J. HAO MODULI: unlocking preference generalization via diffusion models for offline multi-objective reinforcement learning. In Forty-second International Conference on Machine Learning, Cited by: cite148†§1 , cite166†§2 .\nL437:   * Zhang et al. (2025) S. Zhang, W. Zhang, and Q. Gu Energy-weighted flow matching for offline reinforcement learning. In The Thirteenth International Conference on Learning Representations, Cited by: cite156†§1 , cite163†§2 , cite167†§3.2 , cite168†§4.3 , cite169†§5.1 .\nL438:   * Zheng et al. (2023) Q. Zheng, M. Le, N. Shaul, Y. Lipman, A. Grover, and R. T. Chen Guided flows for generative modeling and decision making. arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.13443. Cited by: cite146†§1 , cite163†§2 .\nL439: ## Appendix A Theory and Proofs\nL440: \nL441: ### A.1 Conditional generator derivation and CTMC validity\nL442: \nL443: #### Time derivative of the path.\nL444: \nL445: For any $a^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{A}$, differentiating (cite124†8 ) yields\nL446: \nL447:  | $$\\frac{d}{dt}p_{t\\mid Z}(a^{\\prime})=\\delta(a^{\\prime},A_{1})-\\delta(a^{\\prime},A_{0}).$$  |  | (17)\nL448: \nL449: This derivative is fully determined by the chosen path and involves no modeling choices.\nL450: #### Kolmogorov forward equation.\nL451: \nL452: Let $u_{t}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid Z)$ denote time-dependent CTMC rates. The Kolmogorov equation requires\nL453: \nL454:  | $$\\frac{d}{dt}p_{t\\mid Z}(a^{\\prime})=\\sum_{a\\in\\mathcal{A}}u_{t}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid Z)\\,p_{t\\mid Z}(a).$$  |  | (18)\nL455: \nL456: Since $p_{t\\mid Z}$ has support only on $\\{A_{0},A_{1}\\}$, the sum reduces to\nL457: \nL458:  | $$\\frac{d}{dt}p_{t\\mid Z}(a^{\\prime})=(1-t)\\,u_{t}(a^{\\prime},A_{0}\\mid Z)+t\\,u_{t}(a^{\\prime},A_{1}\\mid Z).$$  |  | (19)\nL459: #### Endpoint-absorbing design choice.\nL460: \nL461: We impose the standard DFM constraint that the endpoint is absorbing (since no jumping to another point as we are at endpoint):\nL462: \nL463:  | $$u_{t}(a^{\\prime},A_{1}\\mid Z)=0\\qquad\\forall\\,a^{\\prime}.$$  |\nL464: \nL465: Under this constraint, (cite170†19 ) becomes\nL466: \nL467:  | $$\\frac{d}{dt}p_{t\\mid Z}(a^{\\prime})=(1-t)\\,u_{t}(a^{\\prime},A_{0}\\mid Z).$$  |  | (20)\nL468: #### Solving for the conditional rates.\nL469: \nL470: Equating (cite171†20 ) with the true derivative (cite172†17 ) gives\nL471: \nL472:  | $$(1-t)\\,u_{t}(a^{\\prime},A_{0}\\mid Z)=\\delta(a^{\\prime},A_{1})-\\delta(a^{\\prime},A_{0}),$$  |\nL473: \nL474: which admits the following valid CTMC generator (jump-to-endpoint) choice\nL475: \nL476:  | $$u_{t}(a^{\\prime},A_{0}\\mid Z)=\\frac{1}{1-t}\\Big(\\delta(a^{\\prime},A_{1})-\\delta(a^{\\prime},A_{0})\\Big).$$  |  | (21)\nL477: Combining this with the absorbing condition at $A_{1}$, the conditional rate kernel can be written compactly as\nL478: \nL479:  | $$u_{t}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid Z)=\\frac{1}{1-t}\\Big(\\delta(a^{\\prime},A_{1})-\\delta(a^{\\prime},a)\\Big).$$  |  | (22)\nL480: \nL481: If the chain is currently at $a$, the only positive off-diagonal rate is a jump toward the endpoint $A_{1}$. The diagonal term is negative to ensure each column sums to zero, as required for valid CTMC rates. Once $A_{1}$ is reached, the process remains there.\nL482: #### Validity of the conditional rate field.\nL483: A central requirement for discrete flow matching is that the conditional rate field $u_{t}(\\cdot,\\cdot\\mid Z)$ defines a valid Continuous-Time Markov Chain (CTMC) generator.\nL484: In particular, for every conditioning variable $Z$ and every current action $a\\in\\mathcal{A}$, the rates must satisfy the generator constraints: (i) non-negativity of off-diagonal entries, $u_{t}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid Z)\\geq 0$ for all $a^{\\prime}\\neq a$, and (ii) conservation of probability mass, $\\sum_{a^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{A}}u_{t}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid Z)=0$.\nL485: While compact expressions for conditional generators are often convenient, they do not make these constraints explicit and may yield negative off-diagonal entries for some $(a^{\\prime},a)$, in which case the resulting operator does not correspond to a valid CTMC generator\nL486: Therefore, to ensure correctness by construction, we explicitly define the conditional rate field in a piecewise form in Eq. (cite125†9 ) that enforces the CTMC constraints for all $t\\in[0,1]$ and all realizations of $Z$. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first energy-guided, preference-conditioned discrete policy optimization framework to explicitly enforce CTMC generator validity at the level of the conditional rate field, rather than relying on implicit or relaxed formulations.\nL487: #### Conditional CTMC rates.\nL488: \nL489: To construct a valid conditional probability flow that exactly interpolates between the source action $A_{0}$ and the target endpoint $A_{1}$, we define a time-dependent CTMC generator whose dynamics concentrate all probability mass at $A_{1}$ as $t\\to 1$ while satisfying the generator constraints by construction. Concretely, we use the following jump-to-endpoint rate field:\nL490:  | $$u_{t}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid Z)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{1-t},&a\\neq A_{1},\\;a^{\\prime}=A_{1},\\\\\nL491: -\\frac{1}{1-t},&a\\neq A_{1},\\;a^{\\prime}=a,\\\\\nL492: 0,&\\text{otherwise},\\end{cases}$$  |\nL493: \nL494: with $A_{1}$ absorbing. This generator induces the desired conditional path and guarantees nonnegative off-diagonal rates and column-wise mass conservation, as required for a valid CTMC (cite111†Gat et al., 2024 ; cite104†Lipman et al., 2024 ).\nL495: ### A.2 Critic Backup and In-Support Approximation Details\nL496: \nL497: This section provides additional details on the critic backup used in our multi-objective offline reinforcement learning setup. The material here is included for completeness and to clarify implementation choices; the main text only relies on the resulting update rule.\nL498: #### Soft Bellman backup under KL regularization.\nL499: \nL500: For a fixed state $s$ and preference vector $\\omega$, the KL-regularized optimal policy has the Boltzmann form\nL501: \nL502:  | $$\\pi^{\\star}_{\\omega}(a\\mid s)\\propto\\mu(a\\mid s)\\exp\\!\\big(\\beta Q_{\\omega}(s,a)\\big),$$  |\nL503: \nL504: where $\\mu(\\cdot\\mid s)$ is the behavior policy and $\\beta>0$ controls the strength of value guidance. Under this policy, the corresponding soft value function is given by the expectation\nL505:  | $$V_{\\omega}(s)=\\mathbb{E}_{a\\sim\\pi^{\\star}_{\\omega}(\\cdot\\mid s)}\\big[Q_{\\omega}(s,a)\\big].$$  |\nL506: \nL507: Substituting the Boltzmann form of $\\pi^{\\star}_{\\omega}$ yields\nL508: \nL509:  | $$V_{\\omega}(s)=\\frac{\\sum_{a\\in\\mathcal{A}}\\mu(a\\mid s)\\exp\\!\\big(\\beta Q_{\\omega}(s,a)\\big)\\,Q_{\\omega}(s,a)}{\\sum_{a\\in\\mathcal{A}}\\mu(a\\mid s)\\exp\\!\\big(\\beta Q_{\\omega}(s,a)\\big)}.$$  |  | (23)\nL510: This expression corresponds to a soft (log-sum-exp–weighted) backup and is standard in KL-regularized reinforcement learning (e.g., cite116†Todorov (2006) ; cite117†Haarnoja et al. (2017) ).\nL511: #### In-support approximation.\nL512: \nL513: In offline reinforcement learning with large or discrete action spaces, computing the full sums in Eq. (cite173†23 ) is typically infeasible. Moreover, evaluating $Q_{\\omega}(s,a)$ for actions outside the support of the dataset can lead to severe extrapolation error.\nL514: To address this, we adopt an in-support approximation in which the expectations are estimated using a finite set of actions sampled from the behavior policy. Specifically, for a given next state $s^{\\prime}$, we sample a support set\nL515: \nL516:  | $$\\mathcal{A}_{\\mathrm{supp}}(s^{\\prime})=\\{a^{\\prime}_{1},\\dots,a^{\\prime}_{M}\\}\\sim\\mu(\\cdot\\mid s^{\\prime}),$$  |\nL517: \nL518: and approximate the soft value as\nL519:  | $$V_{\\omega}(s^{\\prime})\\;\\approx\\;\\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^{M}\\exp\\!\\big(\\hat{\\beta}Q_{\\omega}(s^{\\prime},a^{\\prime}_{j})\\big)\\,Q_{\\omega}(s^{\\prime},a^{\\prime}_{j})}{\\sum_{j=1}^{M}\\exp\\!\\big(\\hat{\\beta}Q_{\\omega}(s^{\\prime},a^{\\prime}_{j})\\big)}.$$  |  | (24)\nL520: This approximation preserves the KL-regularized structure of the backup while ensuring that all evaluated actions lie within the empirical support of the offline dataset. Here $\\hat{\\beta}$ denotes the finite-sample guidance temperature used in the self-normalized Monte Carlo approximation in Eq. (cite122†11 ). In the ideal infinite-support limit it coincides with the theoretical inverse temperature $\\beta$.\nL521: #### Bellman regression target.\nL522: \nL523: Using the approximation above, the scalarized Bellman target used to train the critic is\nL524: \nL525:  | $$y=\\langle\\omega,\\mathbf{r}\\rangle+\\gamma V_{\\omega}(s^{\\prime}),$$  |\nL526: \nL527: where $\\mathbf{r}$ is the observed reward vector. This target is used in a standard squared regression loss for the scalarized critic values, while the underlying vector-valued critic $\\mathbf{Q}_{\\psi}$ is shared across preferences.\nL528: ### A.3 CTMC Inference: Test-Time Sampling for Arbitrary Preferences\nL529: \nL530: After training, the learned CTMC induces a discrete policy $\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid s,\\omega)$ as its terminal distribution. This section describes the Euler-type simulation procedure used to sample actions at test time for arbitrary preference vectors.\nL531: \nL532: Given a state $s$ and a user preference $\\omega$, we generate one action by simulating the CTMC from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\nL533: #### Inputs.\nL534: \nL535: A trained rate model $u_{\\theta,t}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid s,\\omega)$ satisfying the CTMC rate constraints, behavior policy $\\mu(\\cdot\\mid s)$, and a time step $h>0$.\nL536: #### Euler-type CTMC simulation.\nL537: \nL538: We discretize time $n=0,1,\\dots,N\\quad\\text{with }Nh=1$ and simulate one trajectory $(A_{t_{n}})_{n=0}^{N}$ as follows:\nL539: \nL540:   1. 1.\nL541: \nL542: Initialize. Sample an initial action\nL543: \nL544:  | $$A_{0}\\sim\\mu(\\cdot\\mid s)$$  |\nL545: \nL546:   2. 2.\nL547: \nL548: Iterate. For $n=0,1,\\dots,N-1$, given the current action $A_{t_{n}}=a$, compute the total leaving rate\nL549: \nL550:  | $$\\lambda_{n}\\;:=\\;\\sum_{a^{\\prime}\\neq a}u_{\\theta,t_{n}}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid s,\\omega),$$  |\nL551: \nL552: and form the corresponding jump distribution\nL553:  | $$\\rho_{n}(a^{\\prime}\\mid a)\\;:=\\;\\frac{u_{\\theta,t_{n}}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid s,\\omega)}{\\lambda_{n}},\\qquad a^{\\prime}\\neq a,$$  |\nL554: \nL555: (if $\\lambda_{n}=0$, define $\\rho_{n}$ arbitrarily and the chain stays). Then perform one Euler step:\nL556: \nL557:     1. (a)\nL558: \nL559: With probability $1-h\\lambda_{n}$, set $A_{t_{n+1}}=a$ (no jump).\nL560: \nL561:     2. (b)\nL562: \nL563: With probability $h\\lambda_{n}$, draw $A_{t_{n+1}}\\sim\\rho_{n}(\\cdot\\mid a)$ (jump to a new action).\nL564: \nL565:   3. 3.\nL566: \nL567: Output. Return $A_{1}:=A_{t_{N}}$ as the sampled action.\nL568: The resulting $A_{1}$ is a sample from the learned policy $A_{1}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid s,\\omega).$\nL569: #### Step-size condition.\nL570: \nL571: The Euler simulation is valid provided the step size $h>0$ satisfies $h\\,\\lambda_{n}\\leq 1$ for all $n$, ensuring that the jump probability $h\\lambda_{n}$ lies in $[0,1]$. In practice, this can be enforced by choosing $h$ sufficiently small (we use this) or by clamping the total leaving rate.\nL572: #### Remarks.\nL573: \nL574: This simulation is the discrete analogue of integrating an ODE in continuous flow-based policies. The probability of a jump in each step is proportional to the total leaving rate $\\lambda_{n}$; the destination of the jump is chosen proportionally to the off-diagonal rates $u_{\\theta,t_{n}}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid s,\\omega)$. Smaller $h$ yields a more accurate approximation of the continuous-time chain.\nL575: ### A.4 Proof of Proposition cite174†A.1 L576: \nL577: Bregman divergences: Let $\\phi_{x}$ be a differentiable and strictly convex function. The Bregman divergence $D_{x}(y,v)$ is defined as the difference between the value of $\\phi_{x}$ at $y$ and its first-order Taylor approximation at $v$:\nL578: \nL579:  | $$D_{x}(y,v):=\\phi_{x}(y)-\\Big(\\phi_{x}(v)+\\langle\\nabla\\phi_{x}(v),y-v\\rangle\\Big).$$  |  | (25)\nL580: Geometrically, this measures the vertical distance between the convex function $\\phi_{x}$ and its tangent hyperplane at $v$, evaluated at the point $y$.\nL581: ###### Proposition A.1 (Affine invariance of the Bregman gradient).\nL582: \nL583: For any integrable random variable $Y$ and any fixed vector $v$, the expected gradient of the Bregman divergence with respect to $v$ is equal to the gradient of the divergence at the expected value of $Y$:\nL584: \nL585:  | $$\\mathbb{E}_{Y}[\\nabla_{v}D_{x}(Y,v)]=\\nabla_{v}D_{x}(\\mathbb{E}[Y],v).$$  |  | (26)\nL586: ###### Proof.\nL587: \nL588: Step 1: Compute the gradient $\\nabla_{v}D_{x}(y,v)$.\nL589: We differentiate the definition of $D_{x}(y,v)$ with respect to $v$, treating $y$ as a constant.\nL590: \nL591:  | $\\displaystyle\\nabla_{v}D_{x}(y,v)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\nabla_{v}\\Big(\\phi_{x}(y)-\\phi_{x}(v)-\\langle\\nabla\\phi_{x}(v),y-v\\rangle\\Big)$  |\nL592:  |  | $\\displaystyle=0-\\nabla\\phi_{x}(v)-\\nabla_{v}\\Big(\\langle\\nabla\\phi_{x}(v),y-v\\rangle\\Big).$  |\nL593: Using the product rule for the inner product term (or simply expanding the gradient of $\\langle f(v),g(v)\\rangle$):\nL594: \nL595:  | $$\\nabla_{v}\\langle\\nabla\\phi_{x}(v),y-v\\rangle=\\nabla^{2}\\phi_{x}(v)(y-v)-\\nabla\\phi_{x}(v).$$  |\nL596: \nL597: Substituting this back:\nL598:  | $\\displaystyle\\nabla_{v}D_{x}(y,v)$  | $\\displaystyle=-\\nabla\\phi_{x}(v)-\\Big(\\nabla^{2}\\phi_{x}(v)(y-v)-\\nabla\\phi_{x}(v)\\Big)$  |\nL599:  |  | $\\displaystyle=-\\nabla\\phi_{x}(v)-\\nabla^{2}\\phi_{x}(v)(y-v)+\\nabla\\phi_{x}(v)$  |\nL600:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\nabla^{2}\\phi_{x}(v)(v-y).$  |\nL601: \nL602: Step 2: Take the expectation over $Y$.\nL603: Since $v$ is fixed, the Hessian $\\nabla^{2}\\phi_{x}(v)$ is constant with respect to the expectation. By the linearity of expectation:\nL604:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}_{Y}[\\nabla_{v}D_{x}(Y,v)]$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{Y}\\Big[\\nabla^{2}\\phi_{x}(v)(v-Y)\\Big]$  |\nL605:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\nabla^{2}\\phi_{x}(v)\\Big(v-\\mathbb{E}[Y]\\Big).$  |\nL606: \nL607: Step 3: Recognize the form.\nL608: The result $\\nabla^{2}\\phi_{x}(v)(v-\\mathbb{E}[Y])$ is exactly the formula derived in Step 1, but with $\\mathbb{E}[Y]$ replacing $y$. Therefore:\nL609: \nL610:  | $$\\nabla^{2}\\phi_{x}(v)(v-\\mathbb{E}[Y])=\\nabla_{v}D_{x}(\\mathbb{E}[Y],v).$$  |\nL611: \nL612: ∎\nL613: ### A.5 Chain Rule for the Loss (used in Theorem cite129†4.1 )\nL614: \nL615: ###### Proposition A.2 (Chain rule).\nL616: \n"
